course: dictionary
lesson: 3952
title: Funnily, and funnily enough
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Funnily is an adverb, so it tells you how something happens. «He walked funnily after the long ride» means he walked in a strange way, not that he told a joke. Read the words around it to find out what kind of funny is meant.
explain: Funnily also appears in the phrase «funnily enough». Here it means something is surprising, or a strange coincidence. Test your meaning by swapping in "strangely enough". If the sentence still makes sense, you have it.
explain: The example text is about a guinea pig. Its French name is un cochon d'Inde, which word for word is "a pig from India". Then the text says, «Funnily, guinea pigs are not pigs at all, and they come from South America, not from India or from Guinea.» Funnily signals a surprising fact that the name gets wrong.
fact: Funnily is an adverb, so it tells you how something happens.
fact: «Funnily» means in a funny or strange way.
fact: «Funnily enough» means surprisingly, or by a strange coincidence.
fact: Guinea pigs are not pigs at all, and they come from South America.
exercise: «The clown walked funnily across the stage, and everyone laughed.» What does funnily mean? | In a quiet, careful way | Very quickly and happily | Without making any noise | *In a funny or strange way
exercise: Funnily enough, the two friends had the same birthday. What does «funnily enough» show? | A sad ending | A rule that must be obeyed | A question someone is asking | *A surprising coincidence
exercise: Find and copy the word that introduces the surprising fact about guinea pigs. | guinea | *funnily | America | South
test: «He wore his hat funnily, tilted over one ear.» What does funnily describe? | Where he left his hat | *How he wore his hat | When he bought his hat | How much his hat cost
test: Ella says, «Funnily enough, my dog and my cat both like the same toy.» Why does Ella use funnily enough? | To say she is angry about the toy | To ask a question about the toy | To say the toy is broken | *To show a surprising coincidence
exam: «Funnily enough, guinea pigs are not pigs at all.» What does funnily enough tell the reader? | It asks for help | *It warns of a surprising fact | It says the animal is a pig | It says the animal is very large
